<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Virtues" cic="CCC 1803-1845" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">To use after class or in a group setting. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>How does the Catechism define a virtue? Write the definition in your own words.</li>
<li>What are the four cardinal virtues and why are they called that?</li>
<li>What does it mean that prudence is "the charioteer of the virtues"?</li>
<li>Explain the difference between an acquired virtue and an infused virtue. Give an example of each.</li>
<li>What are the three theological virtues? Why can they not be acquired through human effort?</li>
<li>Name at least 5 gifts of the Holy Spirit and 5 fruits of the Holy Spirit.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no "right" answers in the personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Of the four cardinal virtues (prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance), which do you most need to cultivate right now? Why?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be specific: think of a situation in your life where that virtue would help you act better.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Thomas More said before dying: "I die the King's good servant, but God's first." Is there something in your life where you have to choose between pleasing others and being faithful to what you believe?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">It could be something small (staying silent when you should speak, or following the crowd when you know it's wrong).</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Charity is the greatest of all the virtues (1 Cor 13:13). Is there someone you find hard to treat with charity?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Write their name (or initial) and think of a small act of charity you can do for that person this week.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. St. Paul says: "Whatever is true, whatever is honorable, whatever is just, whatever is pure ... think about these things" (Phil 4:8). What kind of content do you consume every day? (Social media? Music? Shows?)</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Do those things help you grow in virtues or distract you from them? Be honest.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. From the twelve fruits of the Holy Spirit, choose ONE you want to see grow in you. Write why you chose it and what you will do this week to cultivate it.</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Examples: peace &mdash; dedicate 5 minutes a day to silence without screens. Patience &mdash; don't complain when something doesn't go as expected.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. The Tree of Virtues</h4>
<p>Draw a large tree on a sheet of paper. In the roots write: "Grace of God" and "Sacraments." On the trunk write: "The Four Cardinal Virtues." On the main branches write: "Faith," "Hope," and "Charity." On the smaller branches write the fruits of the Spirit you already recognize in your life. On the empty branches, write the fruits you don't yet see but would like to see bloom. At the foot of the tree write: "He who abides in me, and I in him, he it is that bears much fruit" (Jn 15:5).</p>

<h4>B. Virtue Check-Up</h4>
<p>Do a brief examination of conscience focused on the virtues. Rate each cardinal virtue in your life NOW from 1 (very weak) to 5 (very strong):</p>
